From 2da6d7f44dc5db9fbf435977077d114108751e06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:01:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/32] Do not forward a load across a call to a procedure that
 is not analysed

InterCodeBasicBlock::DestroyingMem asks the callee whether it stores
through a pointer, but mStoresIndirect means something only once
Close() has collected the procedure's effects and set mGlobalsChecked.
For a recursive call inside a loop the callee is the procedure being
compiled: the flag was still unset (in practice zero), so a value
loaded before the loop was reused after it although the recursive call
had changed it.  ModifiesGlobal() already answers "yes" for such a
procedure; do the same here.  Upstream main does this since 4969258,
which made this test use ModifiesIndirect() (added in 963b29c).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 oscar64/InterCode.cpp | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/oscar64/InterCode.cpp b/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
index 35d27ef..f9bc80c 100644
--- a/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
+++ b/oscar64/InterCode.cpp
@@ -1311,7 +1311,8 @@ bool InterCodeBasicBlock::DestroyingMem(const InterInstruction* lins, const Inte
 
 						if (op.mTemp >= 0)
 						{
-							if (proc->mStoresIndirect)
+							// until Close() has collected them, the effects of a procedure are unknown
+							if (!proc->mGlobalsChecked || proc->mStoresIndirect)
 								return true;
 						}
 						else if (op.mMemory == IM_FFRAME || op.mMemory == IM_FRAME)
